EXT. ABANDONED DRIVE-IN SNACK STAND ROOF - NIGHT

A flat tar roof under a smear of stars. A cracked neon cone sign, unlit. A cooler. Two lawn chairs.

Two teenagers sit with their backs to the sky.

CALLUM ODUYA-LINDQVIST (16, skinny, hoodie, hands tucked in sleeves) watches the road below.

NADEZHDA "NADIA" ABARA-FENWICK (16, cut-off jeans, scab on one knee) lies flat, staring up.

Somewhere far off, a freight train rolls.

NADIA
Seven.

CALLUM
Seven what?

NADIA
Satellites. Since you sat down.

CALLUM
That's not satellites.

NADIA
It's satellites.

CALLUM
It's planes.

NADIA
Planes blink.

He doesn't argue. Leans back. The tar creaks.

CALLUM
My dad's driving back tomorrow.

NADIA
From where?

CALLUM
Reno.

NADIA
What's in Reno?

CALLUM
Nothing. That's the point.

She turns her head toward him.

NADIA
Is he staying?

CALLUM
He says so.

NADIA
Do you believe him?

CALLUM
He bought a couch.

NADIA
That's not the same.

CALLUM
He's never bought a couch.

She sits up. Pulls her knees to her chest. He opens the cooler and offers her a soda. She takes it.

NADIA
Mine's leaving for good, you know.

CALLUM
Your mom?

NADIA
Mine's mom, yes, my mom. Moving to Tucson with a guy named Gordon.

CALLUM
Gordon sounds fake.

NADIA
Gordon sounds like a sofa.

CALLUM
He might be your dad's couch.

She laughs, loud, startling a nighthawk off the sign. Callum smiles, too, for the first time. The freight train's horn fades.

NADIA
You'll still be here.

CALLUM
Yeah.

NADIA
Promise.

CALLUM
I'm not going anywhere. There's nowhere to go.

NADIA
That's not a promise.

CALLUM
It's the only kind I have.

She tips her soda against his. They drink. A shooting star cuts across the sky and neither of them says anything, though both see it.

NADIA
Make a wish.

CALLUM
I did.

NADIA
What was it?

CALLUM
If I tell you, it won't work.

NADIA
That's superstition.

CALLUM
It's a rule.

NADIA
Same thing.

CALLUM
Not tonight.

She lies back. He lies down, too, shoulder to shoulder, and the stars wheel on above them.
